     "result_text_tail": "o answer Inkwell, enterprise cap renegotiation and the new-business plan are out of scope. They're listed in the appendix.\n- The price levels (10%, 8%, 7%) come from the pack's own test, history and contract cap, not from me.\n\n**Numbers:** 149 in total, with no placeholders.\n- 54 are quoted from the briefing pack.\n- 95 are calculated from them, with live formulas in the workbook.\n- 52 of those are my estimates (grade E); the rest are grade B. Grade B is the best possible here, because grade A needs web sources.\n\nAn independent checker re-traced every quoted number and every calculation and found no errors. It did raise logic and wording issues, which I fixed:\n- I had labelled an \"as active as other customers\" case as \"full activation\".\n- I had overstated how the 12% plan compares.\n- A few claims weren't in the pack.\n\nTwo cautions:\n- The path to the 15% target assumes 2027 retention matches the last 12 months, and that those 12 months included no price rise. If enterprise retention already includes contract price escalators, about $0.8M is counted twice. The CFO should confirm this before December.\n- Every churn reaction in the deck is scaled from the 2023 rise, which happened before Inkwell existed.\n\nPossible next steps: fill in the 2027 new-business plan, switch the theme, or add a slide sizing the Q1 SMB test.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `palisade-pricing-2027.pptx`\n- `palisade-pricing-2027-sources.xlsx`\n- `palisade-pricing-2027.json` (the spec the deck is built from)"
    }
